WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
Silic stockings once cost a week's wages. A fiber pulled from coal and air brought the

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.520
price to a few dollars. This is First Principles Daily, episode 91.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.780
Dupont chemists began with the atoms required for a long chain polyamide. They started by

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.660
identifying exactly what atoms had to be linked together to produce the mechanical properties

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:27.600
of a sheer elastic fiber. That meant specifying two six carbon molecules, one carrying acid

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.860
groups at the ends, and the other carrying a mean groups at the ends, so the two could react

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:38.260
repeatedly to form the repeating amide bonds that give the chain its strength and flexibility.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.680
The magic wand floor for those molecules started with commodity benzene from coal or petroleum,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:51.860
ammonia from the harbor process, and oxygen from air. Benzene sold for a few cents per pound at the

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:58.360
time. Ammonia carried a similarly low price. Air supplied the oxygen at essentially the cost of

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:04.560
compression alone. Even after synthesizing a dipic acid and hexamethylene diamine from those

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.940
inputs, the resulting polymer resin sat at a small fraction of the price of realed silk.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:16.540
The idiot index for a silk stocking therefore sat high. The finished garment carried every layer

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.820
of Sarah culture, reeling, shipping, and hand knitting, while the atoms inside the thread

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:27.780
represented only a modest share of that total price. One way to see the gap is to notice how many

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.700
separate operations had to be paid for before any thread reached a knitting needle. Leaf harvesting,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:40.940
worm rearing, cocoon sorting, degumming, twisting, ocean transport, and then the final knitting itself.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.600
Each of those steps added labor and loss that had nothing to do with a carbon,

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:52.460
hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen atoms that ultimately formed the fiber. The first decisive step was a

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:58.100
continuous reactor that converted the intermediates into molten polyamide under controlled temperature

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.400
and pressure. This eliminated batch isolation and purification steps that would have multiplied

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:09.660
handling. Instead of stopping the reaction, isolating the intermediate solids, cleaning equipment between

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.780
batches, and then redesolving or remelting material. The process kept the growing chains in the melt

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:21.080
and move them straight forward. Temperature had to be held within a narrow window so the polymer

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.940
reached the right molecular weight without branching or discoloration, and catalyst residues had to

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.540
stay below levels that would later cause spinnerat blockages. The second step was melt spinning.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:38.140
The polymer was extruded directly through spinnerads whose whole diameter set filament thickness,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:44.080
then drawn to align the molecules and build strength. That single operation replaced the

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:50.380
multi-stage reeling and twisting required for silk. In the silk process, cocoons had to be softened

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:56.600
in hot water, filaments located and collected by hand or machine, several filaments twisted together

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.300
for usable denier, and the resulting yarn wound and rewound multiple times to remove defects.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.040
The melt spinning route produced a continuous filament whose diameter was fixed by the spinnerat

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.640
or of s, and whose orientation was set by the draw ratio applied immediately after extrusion,

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.560
removing those intermediate winding and twisting machines entirely.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:24.260
The third step tuned polymerization degree and draw ratio so one filament could be knitted into

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:29.800
shear fabric without the multiple plies silk needed. Fewer ends per inch cut both knitting

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:36.200
machine time and total yarn consumption. Because the nylon filament already possessed sufficient tensile

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:41.700
strength after drawing, knitters no longer had to feed several fine strands through each needle to

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:47.300
prevent breakage. That reduction in ends per inch lowered the number of yarn packages that had to

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:52.760
be loaded, the number of tension devices that had to be maintained, and the total length of yarn that

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:58.200
passed through the machine for each square matter of fabric. Each of these changes removed a distinct

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:04.000
cost layer that the silkworm model had treated as fixed. The engineering task was to hold temperature

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.000
and catalyst purity tight enough for uniform flow and no degradation in the spinneret.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.960
Once solved, capacity group by adding spinnerets and larger reactors rather than more farms.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:20.660
One might wonder whether the new fiber could still deliver the drape and recovery that silk provided

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:26.540
through its protein structure. The chemists addressed that by selecting a polymer whose hydrogen bonding

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.660
delivered elasticity comparable to silk once the chains were oriented during drawing.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:38.400
Another objection is that coal and ammonia still required upstream processing. Yet those routes

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:44.800
were already amortized across many products, whereas silks dedicated biological infrastructure existed

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.480
only for silk. For centuries, the industry treated the silkworm as an irreplaceable reactor.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.160
Farmers in Japan and China raised the insects, collected cocoons,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:00.360
and sold real thread to weavers and knitters who accepted the biological limits as given.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:07.820
Each added step, leaf cultivation, cocoon sorting, de-gumming, twisting, layered labor and loss.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:14.000
The final yarn stayed scarce enough that one pair of shear stockings could equal several days of factory

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:20.180
wages. Manufacturers refined reeling machines and dye uptake, yet they never questioned whether

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:26.940
protein fiber must come from an insect. When trade tensions in the 1930s cut supply, prices rose

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:32.140
instead of prompting a search for different atoms. Handlers guarded against worm disease,

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:38.680
sorted cocoons for quality, and ran multiple de-gumming baths. Shipping added further cost because

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:44.460
delicate filaments could not be compressed or exposed to moisture. Knitters then combined several

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:49.240
fine strands for usable strength, multiplying the ends that passed through each needle.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.820
Once reactor and spinner at capacity reached steady output, nylon stockings sold for a few dollars.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:02.080
The supply chain part count fell from cocoon handling, reeling, twisting, and ocean shipping to

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.780
chemical synthesis, polymerization, and direct spinning. The new idiot index dropped because

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:13.140
dominant expenses now sat in the chemical plant and knitting machines rather than invariable biological

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:19.360
feedstock. Limits remained, the polymer still required precise moisture control during drawing.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.880
Early nylon yellowed under sunlight until stabilizers were added. Some optical properties of silk

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:31.740
stayed unmatched, which is why certain high-end garments kept silk for decades. The mass market

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:37.640
shifted once nylon proved durable enough for repeated wear and washing. A fiber whose price had

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:43.340
been set by the seasonal rhythms of insect farming revealed its true cost only when the same atoms

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:49.140
were assembled in a continuous chemical line. Scale in the reactor and the spinner at matters more

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:55.400
than incremental improvement of the old biological steps. The same logic that turned coal and air into

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:01.020
stockings now invites anyone facing a scarce natural input to list the required atoms first,

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:07.160
and then design the shortest industrial path between them. What material now treated as a finished

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:13.100
agricultural product could be rebuilt the same way? That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

